<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wei Zifu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/91-bce">91 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · Also known as Empress Xiaowusi, Thoughtful Empress Wei</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wei Zifu was an empress consort of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a>, the second wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu</a>, his spouse for 49 years and his empress for 38, the second longest tenure in Chinese history. Born to a serf family in the household of Princess Pingyang, where she was trained as a singer and dancer, she caught the emperor's eye in 139 BCE and was made empress after bearing his first son, Liu Ju. Her half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wei-qing">Wei Qing</a> and nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huo-qubing">Huo Qubing</a> became leading generals. In 91 BCE, amid the witchcraft persecutions, the official Jiang Chong accused the crown prince; Liu Ju rose against the conspirators with her support and was defeated, and when the emperor had her seal seized she took her own life.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Wei Zifu was the fourth child and youngest daughter of a housemaid in the household of Princess Pingyang, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu</a>'s elder sister; her father presumably died around the time of her birth. Her younger half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wei-qing">Wei Qing</a> was born of her mother's affair with a minor official of the household. She was trained at the estate in dancing and the four arts and was skilled at singing and composing. In the spring of 139 BCE the emperor, visiting his sister after a ritual at Bashang, noticed her among the dancers and took her back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>, with Wei Qing as a stableboy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Empress Chen, the emperor's childless first wife, made him set her aside, and for more than a year she was a neglected palace maid. The emperor saw her again when she joined a line of maids waiting to be sent home, and she soon became pregnant, which silenced talk that he could not father children. After Empress Chen's mother, Princess Guantao, had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wei-qing">Wei Qing</a> kidnapped, the emperor made her a consort and raised her brother to high office. Empress Chen was deposed in 130 BCE for using witchcraft, and soon after Wei Zifu bore the emperor's first son, Liu Ju.</p><p>As empress she was recorded as modest and careful, keeping her clan out of trouble, and the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a> called her fine in virtues. By 123 BCE her family held five marquessates, and a folk song said that Wei Zifu dominated the world. Even after the emperor came to favour other consorts, he left the palaces and the Shaofu in her charge when he was away from the capital.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 91 BCE the witchcraft trials removed most of her son's allies, among them her brother-in-law the prime minister and his son, two of her daughters and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wei-qing">Wei Qing</a>'s eldest son Wei Kang. After Liu Ju's rising failed in five days of street fighting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>, the emperor sent officials to seize her seal, and she killed herself on 9 September 91 BCE. She was buried in a small coffin outside the Fu'ang Gate. Her great-grandson Liu Bingyi, who survived as an infant in prison, became Emperor Xuan in 74 BCE, cleared her name, built her a larger mausoleum and gave her the title Thoughtful Empress Wei.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wei Zifu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Wei Qing, the son of a servant woman in a princess’s household, worked as a sheepherder and stableboy before his half-sister <b>Wei Zifu</b> became Emperor Wu’s empress; he rose to be Grand General and led the armies against the Xiongnu.</blockquote><a href="/societies/han-china">Social Hierarchy of Han China</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.31,108.86&amp;year=-101" aria-label="Chang'an on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Wei Zifu"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4218 4594h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4218 4594h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=34.31,108.86&amp;year=-101">What was here in 101 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
